8bc62-8564-5c18-b969-026f902a99df" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475894 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186475569 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475894 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186475626 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475894 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186475642 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475894 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186475643 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475894 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186475688 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475894 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475894 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475894 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186475643 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475894 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d25ac4d7-d0a8-5f24-a449-c058b7541cbd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475918 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186475894 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475918 #edb/ref 1069 "q-51577" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475918 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475918 #edb/ref 1071 "Classify the polynomial $7{z}^{3}$ according to its degree and the number of terms." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475918 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "17858799-d724-5cb1-b5ca-0839a5bde618" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475918 #edb/ref 1074 "The polynomial $7{z}^{3}$ has only one term, so it is a monomial.\n\nAlso, its degree is $3,$ so it is a cubic.\n\nTherefore, the polynomial is a cubic monomial." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475925 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186475863 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475925 #edb/ref 1069 "q-152438" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475925 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475925 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true? \n\nI. The product of $7x$ and $3{x}^{2}$ is a binomial.\n\nII. The product of $4x$ and $5$ is a monomial.\n\nIII. The product of two monomials is **always** a binomial." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475925 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "298a3ab5-9f3b-5907-82bb-2239de27222c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475925 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have\n\n$$\n(7x)\\times (3{x}^{2})=21{x}^{3},\n$$\n\nwhich is *not* a binomial (it's a monomial).\n\n- Statement II is true. We have\n\n$$\n(4x)\\times (5)=20x,\n$$\n\nwhich is a monomial.\n\n- Statement III is false. There are products of two monomials that are not binomials (see, for example, statement I and II).\n\nIn fact, the product of two monomials is *never* a binomial.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475927 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186475865 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475927 #edb/ref 1069 "q-152252" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475927 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475927 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true? \n\nI. $5{x}^{2}-7x+1$ is cubic trinomial.\n\nII. ${x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+x$ is cubic trinomial.\n\nIII. **Some** trinomials are cubic." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475927 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2c70515f-461b-5406-a00b-651a86f2659d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475927 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since the degree of the trinomial $5{x}^{2}-7x+1$ is $2,$ it is a quadratic trinomial.\n\n- Statement II is true. Since the degree of the trinomial ${x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+x$ is $3,$ it is a cubic trinomial.\n\n- Statement III is true. A trinomial *could be* cubic (see, for example, statement II). \n\n However, a trinomial is *not always* cubic (see, for example, statement I). \n\n So, some trinomials are cubic.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475965 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186475889 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475965 #edb/ref 1069 "q-44689" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475965 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475965 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following expressions is a trinomial?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475965 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c458355f-4593-5f0b-9073-264d9e39547a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475965 #edb/ref 1074 "The polynomial $8{v}^{4}-10v+19$ has three terms, so it is a trinomial." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475967 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186475887 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475967 #edb/ref 1069 "q-7093" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475967 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475967 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following expressions is a binomial?\n\nI. ${x}^{2}+x+4$\n\nII. ${x}^{3}+2x$\n\nIII. $2{x}^{2}+5$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475967 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cbfb11c2-4e8c-533c-b361-e0d38d677419" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475967 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's analyze each expression.\n\n- Expression I is not a binomial. The polynomial ${x}^{2}+x+4$ has three terms, so it is a trinomial, not a binomial.\n\n- Expression II is a binomial. The polynomial ${x}^{3}+2x$ has two terms, so it is indeed a binomial.\n\n- Expression III is a binomial. The polynomial $2{x}^{2}+5$ has two terms, so it is indeed a binomial.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475973 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186475856 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475973 #edb/ref 1069 "q-111225" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475973 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475973 #edb/ref 1071 "Categorize the polynomial $3{h}^{4}$ based on its degree and the number of terms it has." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475973 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e00081d5-c6bc-5ebe-9c4d-19fd46daf244" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475973 #edb/ref 1074 "The polynomial $3{h}^{4}$ has only one term, so it is a monomial.\n\nAlso, its degree is $4,$ so it is a quartic.\n\nTherefore, the polynomial is a quartic monomial." #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
